WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.417 --> 00:00:01.543
—Okay. 
—Okay.

00:00:01.543 --> 00:00:02.961
Okay, we've got something to tell you—

00:00:02.961 --> 00:00:03.920
... about a new way—

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:05.130
...to support Vox video.

00:00:06.673 --> 00:00:08.550
But first, we want to tell you 
a little bit

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:10.343
about what makes us... us.

00:00:11.302 --> 00:00:13.638
When you get to the end of a video, 
you see this.

00:00:13.847 --> 00:00:15.932
All of these people make Vox video...

00:00:15.974 --> 00:00:16.766
what it is.

00:00:16.766 --> 00:00:18.309
First, you've got the producer.

00:00:18.351 --> 00:00:20.895
The producer usually comes up with 
the idea for the video.

00:00:21.062 --> 00:00:22.272
We make phone calls.

00:00:22.272 --> 00:00:23.732
We read research papers. 

00:00:23.732 --> 00:00:25.900
We watch also a lot of YouTube. 

00:00:25.984 --> 00:00:26.818
Pitches it. 

00:00:26.818 --> 00:00:27.736
Reports it out. 

00:00:27.861 --> 00:00:30.405
So we interviewed 
20 different experts

00:00:30.405 --> 00:00:33.074
to find the ideal candidate 
for our debate video

00:00:33.074 --> 00:00:34.909
on euthanasia policy in Canada.

00:00:35.118 --> 00:00:36.244
This chart shows how many charts

00:00:36.244 --> 00:00:37.620
I'm going to make for 
the next video.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:39.247
Is 500 charts too many?

00:00:40.165 --> 00:00:41.082
I am...

00:00:42.876 --> 00:00:44.961
I don't feel like I'm worthy to be 
the charts guy.

00:00:45.003 --> 00:00:46.296
Do you know what I mean, like—

00:00:46.296 --> 00:00:49.549
I like to make them because I like them 
as a communication tool.

00:00:49.883 --> 00:00:50.425
But—

00:00:51.551 --> 00:00:52.385
Writes the script.

00:00:52.802 --> 00:00:55.597
and we're the ones that you're usually
hearing in the video.

00:00:55.597 --> 00:00:56.306
And then—

00:00:56.306 --> 00:00:58.683
I had ham and cheese croissant.

00:00:59.184 --> 00:01:00.101
Okay, let me explain.

00:01:00.101 --> 00:01:02.353
We also do the mic check sometimes.

00:01:02.353 --> 00:01:05.315
And you don't want to waste 
a good question on a mic check.

00:01:05.565 --> 00:01:07.734
So ask them a fake question, 
which is often...

00:01:08.151 --> 00:01:10.028
what did you have for breakfast 
that day?

00:01:10.737 --> 00:01:11.738
Oatmeal cookies.

00:01:11.738 --> 00:01:13.239
Overnight oats for breakfast.

00:01:13.239 --> 00:01:14.616
Cinnamon toast.

00:01:14.616 --> 00:01:16.576
Because I am a four year old.

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:18.620
How's the audio?

00:01:19.996 --> 00:01:21.372
You'll often see a researcher.

00:01:21.498 --> 00:01:23.166
I spend hours poring over research

00:01:23.166 --> 00:01:24.876
and calling different experts, too.

00:01:24.876 --> 00:01:26.628
To make sure that we're 
not just relying

00:01:26.628 --> 00:01:28.296
on what we find online 
for a fact.

00:01:28.338 --> 00:01:31.424
Sometimes when we need to 
map something out for Vox Atlas

00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:33.802
the information isn't easily accessible.

00:01:33.885 --> 00:01:34.928
For all these maps

00:01:34.928 --> 00:01:37.388
I spoke to dozens and dozens of experts...

00:01:37.597 --> 00:01:39.057
to get every detail right.

00:01:39.099 --> 00:01:41.017
Sometimes we'll know 
something anecdotally

00:01:41.017 --> 00:01:44.020
but we'll need evidence to back it up
and to show it on screen.

00:01:44.145 --> 00:01:46.481
So for the video on Texas judges, 
for example...

00:01:46.731 --> 00:01:49.400
we had to track down court cases

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:52.570
experts and data to back up 
what we're seeing.

00:01:52.612 --> 00:01:55.782
Story editors make sure that 
a video script makes sense

00:01:55.782 --> 00:01:57.033
and that the entire story

00:01:57.033 --> 00:01:59.119
is going to deliver a really solid explanation.

00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:01.871
We ask questions, 
we suggest rewrites

00:02:01.871 --> 00:02:03.373
and generally we try to cut out

00:02:03.373 --> 00:02:04.749
all the stuff that doesn't need to be there.

00:02:04.958 --> 00:02:06.167
Then, there's the editor.

00:02:06.251 --> 00:02:08.628
This is who builds the video in Adobe Premiere.

00:02:09.003 --> 00:02:09.796
Assuming it doesn't—

00:02:10.004 --> 00:02:10.880
Hmm. [Beep]

00:02:11.005 --> 00:02:12.132
Assuming it doesn't crash.

00:02:12.382 --> 00:02:13.216
He can jump cut that. 

00:02:13.508 --> 00:02:16.719
This part plays a huge role 
in shaping how a video feels.

00:02:16.761 --> 00:02:18.596
It's the difference between this...

00:02:18.596 --> 00:02:19.222
[country-inspired song plays]

00:02:19.222 --> 00:02:21.266
[singing] This here’s a song for 
a truck driving mother—

00:02:21.516 --> 00:02:22.225
And this...

00:02:22.225 --> 00:02:23.226
[pensive, plucking string music]

00:02:23.226 --> 00:02:25.186
Sound design makes archival 
come to life.

00:02:25.395 --> 00:02:26.729
It gives a voice to photos

00:02:26.729 --> 00:02:27.814
and power to videos. 

00:02:28.064 --> 00:02:30.066
And with sound effects on our animations...

00:02:30.275 --> 00:02:32.986
we can highlight every newspaper, 
calculate any chart...

00:02:33.153 --> 00:02:36.114
click every web page and make sure 
every robot has the right...

00:02:36.114 --> 00:02:37.282
[robotic beeping]

00:02:37.866 --> 00:02:40.326
Sometimes it's just watching 
a ton of archive footage

00:02:40.326 --> 00:02:42.579
looking for the perfect shot 
to match the VO.

00:02:42.662 --> 00:02:45.165
The animators make the video come alive.

00:02:45.165 --> 00:02:46.958
Starting with rough sketches

00:02:46.958 --> 00:02:49.836
and refining with each 
and every draft.

00:02:49.836 --> 00:02:51.129
We are making it pretty

00:02:51.129 --> 00:02:53.756
but we're also making it make sense.

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:57.010
This is how we animate a yellow highlight 
in a couple of clicks.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:01.306
When we have a video cut

00:03:01.306 --> 00:03:04.517
we go through many, many rounds of notes 
before we publish.

00:03:04.559 --> 00:03:07.812
Our art director makes sure that 
the visuals are on point.

00:03:08.354 --> 00:03:10.607
Our researcher checks the facts again.

00:03:10.773 --> 00:03:13.818
And this is where we really celebrate
every tiny catch.

00:03:13.818 --> 00:03:15.528
Copy editors make sure we spell good.

00:03:15.945 --> 00:03:18.448
And because we have multiple videos 
in production every day.

00:03:18.489 --> 00:03:19.908
These are all the people that keep it moving.

00:03:20.116 --> 00:03:24.245
And last but not least, I get the video
ready for YouTube.

00:03:24.412 --> 00:03:25.914
I make all the subtitles 
[hey that’s me! right here!]

00:03:25.914 --> 00:03:28.208
and then I read all your comments.

00:03:28.458 --> 00:03:30.460
All of them.
[say hi in the comments below!]

00:03:31.002 --> 00:03:32.212
And then there's you.

00:03:33.004 --> 00:03:35.715
You support us every time 
you watch one of our videos.

00:03:35.715 --> 00:03:38.051
Now there's another way
you can support us.

00:03:38.051 --> 00:03:39.052
And support our goal

00:03:39.052 --> 00:03:40.845
of making sure that good journalism

00:03:40.845 --> 00:03:43.973
that's also fun to watch 
is never behind a paywall.

00:03:44.015 --> 00:03:45.725
We keep our work here 
for free.

00:03:45.808 --> 00:03:48.269
So our journalism can be accessed 
anywhere in the world.

00:03:48.311 --> 00:03:49.604
We can see the impact of that choice

00:03:49.604 --> 00:03:51.689
any time one of our videos
makes a difference.

00:03:51.731 --> 00:03:54.067
Like the time one of our 
COVID vaccine videos

00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:56.486
was screened at a vaccine center 
in Taiwan.

00:03:56.486 --> 00:03:57.820
Or all the times that you tell us

00:03:57.820 --> 00:03:59.948
our work has helped you understand 
something better.

00:04:00.156 --> 00:04:01.407
And when teachers let us know

00:04:01.407 --> 00:04:03.952
that they've played our videos 
in their classrooms.

00:04:03.952 --> 00:04:07.372
You can go to vox.com/give-now

00:04:07.372 --> 00:04:10.750
to make a contribution and 
help keep all of this free. 

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:14.045
And help us do more ambitious journalism for you.

00:04:14.796 --> 00:04:16.089
Like, remember this video

00:04:16.089 --> 00:04:17.924
about mysterious circles
in the Sahara?

00:04:18.174 --> 00:04:18.967
“There they were.”

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:20.718
We spent the better part of a year 

00:04:20.718 --> 00:04:23.179
investigating this weird thing that 
we found in Google Earth

00:04:23.179 --> 00:04:24.639
and we actually figured it out.

00:04:24.639 --> 00:04:25.807
“This is crazy.”

00:04:25.807 --> 00:04:28.393
“This is so much more wild than I expected.”

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:29.727
We're making another one.

00:04:29.727 --> 00:04:30.561
In about a week

00:04:30.561 --> 00:04:33.690
I'm leaving for Uruguay to investigate 
a strange forest

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:35.108
with a fascinating story behind it.

00:04:35.149 --> 00:04:37.735
I'm looking for a crew 
to shoot with me in Vietnam.

00:04:37.735 --> 00:04:40.905
And we're trying to figure out what our next 
fact-checked debate should cover.

00:04:40.989 --> 00:04:44.742
I had a protein bar which is 
not a good breakfast.

00:04:46.411 --> 00:04:47.620
But you know, that's...

00:04:48.371 --> 00:04:49.872
We also want to keep doing 
more of the things

00:04:49.872 --> 00:04:50.540
we know you love.

00:04:50.581 --> 00:04:54.252
What's the history behind how 
so many Vietnamese-Americans

00:04:54.252 --> 00:04:55.461
cornered the nail business?

00:04:55.503 --> 00:04:58.756
Why can't New York City solve 
its embarrassing trash problem?

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:00.800
I'll be explaining how movies get made.

00:05:01.134 --> 00:05:03.011
We’re gonna be looking at the Supreme Court
and the court system.

00:05:03.011 --> 00:05:05.805
We’re going to looking at the state governments
or what's happening in state governments.

00:05:05.805 --> 00:05:08.975
We're going to be looking at the election
that apparently is upcoming

00:05:08.975 --> 00:05:11.019
and has very, very old candidates.

00:05:11.102 --> 00:05:14.022
And I can't wait to make the next Atlas episodes

00:05:14.022 --> 00:05:15.565
on Tibet and other places.

00:05:15.565 --> 00:05:17.108
And we have so many stories

00:05:17.108 --> 00:05:19.485
about science and the natural world 
on the way.

00:05:19.527 --> 00:05:21.863
Plus a bunch more beautiful animations

00:05:21.863 --> 00:05:24.240
and interesting visual storytelling.

00:05:24.324 --> 00:05:25.783
We're part of a bigger company

00:05:25.783 --> 00:05:28.536
and our work is partly supported 
by advertising

00:05:28.536 --> 00:05:30.038
and sometimes grants...

00:05:30.288 --> 00:05:32.999
but it's not enough to keep 
this whole machine working.

00:05:33.041 --> 00:05:33.916
We're turning to you.

00:05:33.916 --> 00:05:36.336
No newsroom relies on only 
one funding source.

00:05:36.336 --> 00:05:37.962
Your support will go a long way

00:05:37.962 --> 00:05:40.965
to help us make more Vox videos 
and keep them free for all.

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:43.885
And as a special thank you to those 
who make recurring contributions

00:05:43.885 --> 00:05:45.970
you'll get access to video tutorials

00:05:45.970 --> 00:05:47.805
a newsletter that takes you behind the scenes...

00:05:48.097 --> 00:05:51.642
and Q&amp;A with Vox journalists 
from across our entire newsroom.

00:05:51.893 --> 00:05:54.854
We've been doing this 
for almost ten years now.

00:05:54.854 --> 00:05:57.065
But there's still so much we want 
to make for you.

00:05:57.065 --> 00:06:00.360
So help keep our journalism top notch 
and free for everyone

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:02.111
by becoming a Vox contributor.

00:06:02.236 --> 00:06:05.198
You can go to vox.com/give-now.

00:06:06.657 --> 00:06:07.450
Okay.

00:06:07.533 --> 00:06:07.950
Bye!

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:08.743
I'm going to go.

00:06:08.951 --> 00:06:10.620
I actually really do have to 
get back to my edit. 

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:12.080
[country-inspired music plays]

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:12.455
Thank you! 

00:06:12.455 --> 00:06:13.581
So are we done?

00:06:14.290 --> 00:06:17.251
[yes, this is a country-inspired song for Mother’s day]

00:06:17.877 --> 00:06:23.174
[you can find it on APM music, titled
“Happy Mothers Trukkin Day”]

